al)","summary":"Principality of Serbia (Serbian: Кнежевина Србија/Kneževina Srbija) was one of the early medieval states of the Serbs, located in the western regions of Southeastern Europe. It existed from the 8th century up to c. 969–971 and was ruled by the Vlastimirović dynasty.\nIts first ruler known by name was Višeslav, who started ruling around 780, while by that time (starting from the years 680–681), the Bulgarian state had taken the lands to the east."},{"qid":"Q1311990","from_t":1084,"to_t":1217,"method":"name-alias+date","confidence":0.8047188340141873,"label":"Grand Principality of Serbia","description":"Medieval principality in Southeastern Europe","inception":1084,"dissolved":1217,"enwiki":"Grand Principality of Serbia","summary":"The Grand Principality of Serbia (Serbian: Великожупанска Србија / Velikožupanska Srbija), also known by the anachronistic exonym Rascia (Serbian: Рашка / Raška), was a medieval Serbian state that existed from the second half of the 11th century up until 1217, when it was transformed into the Kingdom of Serbia. After the Grand Principality of Serbia emerged, it gradually expanded during the 12th century, encompassing various neighbouring regions, including territories of Raška (Serbian Cyrillic: Рашка; Latin: Rascia), modern Montenegro, Herzegovina, and southern Dalmatia. It was founded by Grand Prince Vukan, who initially served as the regional governor of the principality (c. 1082), appointed by King Constantine Bodin."},{"qid":"Q2415003","from_t":1217,"to_t":1300,"method":"name-template+date","confidence":0.8744386594600325,"label":"Kingdom of Serbia","description":"1217–1346 Medieval kingdom in Eastern Europe","inception":1217,"dissolved":1346,"enwiki":"Kingdom of Serbia (1217–1346)","summary":"The Kingdom of Serbia (Serbian: Краљевина Србија / Kraljevina Srbija, or the Serbian Kingdom (Serbian: Српско краљевство / Srpsko kraljevstvo), also known as Kingdom of Serbs (Serbian: Краљевина Срба / Kraljevina Srba); Latin: Regnum Serbiæ; Greek: Βασίλειο της Σερβίας), also known by historical exonym Rascia (Serbian: Рашка / Raška), was a medieval Serbian kingdom in Southern Europe comprising most of what is today Serbia (excluding Vojvodina), Kosovo, and Montenegro, as well as southeastern Bosnia and Herzegovina, parts of coastal Croatia south of the Neretva river (excluding Dubrovnik), Albania north of the Drin River, North Macedonia, and a small part of western Bulgaria.\nThe medieval Kingdom of Serbia existed from 1217 to 1346 and was ruled by the Nemanjić dynasty. The Grand Principality of Serbia was elevated with the regal coronation of Stefan the First-Crowned as king, after the reunification of Serbian lands. In 1219, the Serbian Orthodox Church was reorganized as an autocephalous archbishopric, headed by Saint Sava."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q13107370","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q917814","value_label":"Stari Ras","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Stari Ras"},{"qid":"Q13107370","kind":"official_language","value_qid":"Q9299","value_label":"Serbian","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P37","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Serbian"},{"qid":"Q13107370","kind":"religion","value_qid":"Q201571","value_label":"Slavic mythology","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P140","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Slavic mythology"},{"qid":"Q13107370","kind":"government","value_qid":"Q7269","value_label":"monarchy","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P122","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"monarchy"},{"qid":"Q1311990","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q917814","value_label":"Stari Ras","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Stari Ras"},{"qid":"Q1311990","kind":"official_language","value_qid":"Q9299","value_label":"Serbian","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P37","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Serbian"},{"qid":"Q1311990","kind":"language","value_qid":"Q35078